---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Calorie Calculator – How Many Calories Should I Eat? | Nawtch"
  description="Free calorie calculator — find your daily calorie deficit, maintenance, or surplus target based on your age, weight, height, and activity level."
  canonicalPath="/calculators/calorie/"
  bodyClass="calculators"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Calorie Calculator</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Find out how many calories you should eat per day.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/calculators/">← Back to Calculators</a>
  </header>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        If you're wondering <strong>"how many calories should I eat per day?"</strong> — the honest answer
        depends on your body and your goal, but this calculator gives you a solid, personalized starting number
        in under a minute. Enter your age, height, weight, and how active you are, plus whether you want to
        lose weight, gain weight, or stay where you are, and you'll get a daily calorie range to aim for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="calc-tdee"></div>

  <div id="calc-tdee-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        We estimate your BMR — the calories your body burns just staying alive at rest — using the <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor equation</strong>, the formula considered most accurate for healthy adults. We
        then multiply that by how active you are day-to-day to get your full daily calorie burn, and shift it up
        or down based on whether you want to lose, maintain, or gain weight.
        <br /><br />
        This number goes by a more technical name in fitness and nutrition science: <strong>TDEE</strong>, or
        Total Daily Energy Expenditure. If you're already familiar with that term, want to see BMR and TDEE
        broken out separately instead of just the final range, or want a fuller explanation of what each
        activity level actually means, check out our <a href="/calculators/tdee/">TDEE Calculator</a>.
      </p>
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original study →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is a Calorie Deficit/Surplus?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        A <strong>calorie deficit</strong> just means eating fewer calories than your body burns in a day —
        when that happens, your body makes up the difference by using stored energy (body fat), which is the
        basic mechanism behind weight loss. A <strong>calorie surplus</strong> is the opposite: eating more than
        you burn, which supports weight gain over time. Eating roughly the same number of calories you burn —
        your maintenance level — keeps your weight fairly stable. The range the calculator gives you above is
        already built around whichever one of these fits your goal, so you don't need to calculate it yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How Many Calories Should I Eat to Lose Weight?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        There's no single number that works for everyone — that's why this calculator asks for your details
        instead of giving a flat answer. As a rough reference point, one pound of body weight is often
        estimated at around 3,500 calories, a figure that traces back to research from the 1950s.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13594881/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original paper →</a>
        <br /><br />
        More recent research has shown this is a useful approximation rather than an exact rule — your body
        adjusts somewhat as you lose weight, so results aren't perfectly linear week to week.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21872751/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the study →</a>
        <br /><br />
        In practice, this means a moderate, steady calorie deficit is generally easier to sustain and easier to
        predict than a very aggressive one, which is part of why the ranges above avoid extreme numbers by
        default.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Things to Know Before You Start</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        A few things that trip up a lot of people the first time they use a number like this:
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Your activity level is easy to overestimate.</strong> Most people round up when guessing how
        active they really are day to day, which pushes the calorie number higher than it should be. If your
        results don't seem to be lining up with your weight over a few weeks, it's worth re-checking that input
        first.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Weight loss isn't perfectly steady.</strong> Water retention, hormones, sleep, and sodium intake
        can all move the scale by a pound or two in either direction on any given day, even when you're doing
        everything "right." Look at the trend over a couple of weeks rather than judging by any single day.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>The number isn't meant to be permanent.</strong> As you lose or gain weight, the calories your
        body needs change too, so it's normal — and expected — to recalculate every so often rather than
        treating one result as fixed forever.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script src="../../../islands/vanilla/calculators/calorie/calorie.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
